---

<Article
  title="Forest Shuffle Expansions: Alpine vs Woodland Edge vs Exploration"
  description="What each Forest Shuffle expansion adds, which one to buy first, whether you can mix them, and whether everything still fits in the base game box."
  game="forest-shuffle"
  gameTitle="Forest Shuffle"
  category="Guide"
  product="forest-shuffle-insert"
  publishDate="2026-11-20"
>
  <p>
    Forest Shuffle has three expansions, and all of them are small decks you
    shuffle into the base game. None of them adds a board or a new phase, so
    the question isn't "is it worth the complexity?" but "which new cards do
    I want in my forest?" Here's what each one brings.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Buy Alpine first if you want more of the same with nothing new to learn. Pick Woodland Edge for a small twist: bushes give your animals and plants new places to go. Get Exploration if you play solo or want caves with their own powers. All three shuffle into the base deck, and all three fit in the base game box, even with sleeves." />

  <ComparisonTable title="The three expansions at a glance">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Expansion</th>
          <th>Released</th>
          <th>Cards</th>
          <th>What's new</th>
          <th>Best for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Alpine</strong></td>
          <td>2024</td>
          <td>36</td>
          <td>Two new tree species and mountain animals and plants</td>
          <td>More variety, nothing new to learn</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Woodland Edge</strong></td>
          <td>2024</td>
          <td>36</td>
          <td>Bushes: new cards your species can be played around</td>
          <td>A small new rule</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Exploration</strong></td>
          <td>2025</td>
          <td>55</td>
          <td>Caves with powers, a solo Automa deck with 12 challenges, 15 special cards</td>
          <td>Solo players and regulars</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Alpine: more forest, nothing new to learn</h2>
  <p>
    Alpine was the first expansion, with 36 cards. It adds two new tree
    species, the European larch and the Swiss stone pine, along with mountain
    animals and plants such as the golden eagle, marmot, chamois, edelweiss
    and gentian.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are no new rules to speak of. You shuffle the cards in and, during
    setup, remove a few more cards depending on the number of players, so the
    game doesn't run longer. If your group knows the base game well and just
    wants fresh combinations, this is the easy pick.
  </p>

  <h2>Woodland Edge: bushes</h2>
  <p>
    Woodland Edge is also 36 cards: 12 bushes, plus 24 animal and plant cards
    (12 that go above or below, 12 that go to the left or right). Bushes are
    the new idea. Like a tree,
    a bush offers spots where you can place your species. But a bush isn't a
    tree, and some cards care about that, so it changes how you plan your
    forest a little.
  </p>
  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Don't add Alpine and Woodland Edge together at first">
    You can shuffle both expansion decks into the base game at once, but
    Lookout recommends against it for your first few games with them. Learn
    one, then mix.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Exploration: solo play, new caves and rare cards</h2>
  <p>
    Exploration is the biggest box, with 55 cards, and the only one that makes
    Forest Shuffle playable alone (1–5 players instead of 2–5). It has three
    parts:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>An Automa deck for solo play</strong> with 12 challenges, so a
      solo game has a goal beyond beating your own score.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Caves with their own powers.</strong> You choose them at the
      start instead of the plain caves, which gives each player a different
      angle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>15 special cards</strong> of rare species that were only
      available as promos before.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It's the least "more of the same" of the three. If you play with a group
    that's happy with the base game, the caves are the part you'll notice. If
    you play alone, it's the one to get.
  </p>

  <h2>Dartmoor is not an expansion</h2>
  <p>
    Forest Shuffle: Dartmoor (2025) is a standalone game with its own 180
    cards, not an expansion for the original. Don't buy it expecting more
    cards for your existing box.
  </p>

  <h2>Which one to buy first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You play with 2–5 people and want variety:</strong> Alpine.</li>
    <li><strong>You've played the base game a lot and want a twist:</strong> Woodland Edge.</li>
    <li><strong>You play solo, or want something new for every player:</strong> Exploration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Does it all fit in the base game box?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes. Lookout says the Alpine and Woodland Edge cards fit in the base game
    box even when sleeved, and that the Exploration deck still fits too. With
    everything in, that's 307 cards in one box: 180 from the base game, 36
    from Alpine, 36 from Woodland Edge and 55 from Exploration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fitting is the easy part. Finding the winter cards, the caves and the
    Automa deck in a box of 307 cards is harder. Our
    <a href="/games/forest-shuffle/forest-shuffle-storage/">storage guide</a>
    shows how to sort it all.
  </p>
</Article>
